----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCOPE — what shipped (parity with the web source)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The web `WidgetGaugeHero` is a PURE presentational widget primitive (a shared widget building block): it
  takes a `gauge: GaugeHeroConfig` (`value` / `max` / `label` / `unit` / `color`) plus optional
  `stats: GaugeHeroStat[]`, `compact`, and `children`, and renders a centered column: ALWAYS a
  `<RadialGauge>` (the embedded ring: clamped = `Math.max(0, Math.min(value, max))`, arc fraction =
  `clamped / max`, decimals = `Number.isInteger(clamped) ? 0 : globalPrecision(2)`, the formatted reading +
  unit at the center, the caption below, `size = compact ? 70 : 100`, `STROKE_WIDTH = 8`), then — only when
  `!compact && stats.length > 0` — a centered wrapping stats row, then — only when `!compact && children` —
  the accessory slot.
  There is NO fetch / React-Query / Promise in the source, so it has NO loading/error/stale/offline branch
  (the host widget that owns the query renders those). Per the sibling presentational-primitive precedent
  (WidgetComparisonCard 0003, WidgetStatGrid 0010, MetricCard 0095) this surface reproduces ONLY the
  source's REAL branches — inventing loading/error/stale/offline chrome would fabricate states the source
  does not have:
    • the always-present hero ring   (web always renders `<RadialGauge>`)
    • the standard + stats row        (web `!compact && stats.length > 0`)
    • the standard + accessory slot   (web `!compact && children` → a generic `@ViewBuilder Accessory`)
    • the compact ring                (web `compact ? 70 : 100`; stats + accessory suppressed)
  There is deliberately NO empty leaf — the gauge ring is ALWAYS the content, so the surface never renders a
  blank box even with zero stats. A `max <= 0` config is guarded to a zero-fill ring (native null-safety:
  the web `clamped / max` would divide by zero -> NaN arc). The web `gauge.color` string is modeled as a
  theme-aware semantic `GaugeTint` token (no raw hex), exactly as WidgetStatGrid (0010) maps `valueColor?`
  and MetricCard (0095) maps `neonColorMap[color]`. The web `fmtNumber` + integer-aware decimals rule is
  ported in `GaugeValueFormatter` (locale injected: device locale at runtime, en_US pinned in tests).
  Files: WidgetGaugeHero.Adapter/Model/.swift/Views/Previews/AdapterTests/Tests.swift + .strings.
  Bindings : P1/S8 WidgetGaugeHeroModel @Observable holder (props + precision + locale -> projection; no HTTP
             in the view); P1/S9 tokens (Color.TS/Font.TS/TSSpacing/TSRadius/TSAnimation) — no Tailwind ports,
             no raw hex; P1/S10 NSLocalizedString facade (table "WidgetGaugeHero"); P1/S11 view.opened
             telemetry (slug "WidgetGaugeHero", emitted once + idempotent across appear/disappear).
  i18n     : web source (incl. RadialGauge) has 0 t() keys AND 0 own literal copy — every visible string
             (gauge.label/unit, each stat label/value/unit) is a caller-supplied, already-localized prop
             rendered verbatim. All 4 catalog keys are native a11y/HIG additions: valueUnit, gaugeLabel,
             gaugeValue (spoken percent), statLabel. 0 dead keys; 0 hardcoded English in Swift.
  a11y     : the ring folds caption + value(+unit) into one VoiceOver element with the whole-percent fill as
             its spoken accessibility value ("{label}, {value}{unit}" / "{n}% of maximum"); the decorative
             track + arc + center text are ignored by VoiceOver; each stat cell folds label+value(+unit) into
             one element; the arc animation honors Reduce Motion (TSAnimation.slow); Dynamic Type via tokens;
             high-contrast via the dynamic token colors; center reading uses minimumScaleFactor to avoid
             clipping in the condensed ring.

